#!/opt/homebrew/bin/python3 -I
"""review-launch — the root-owned launcher `agents` reaches through one sudo
rule to request a review (platform/decisions/0090,
platform/specs/builder-runs-as-two-accounts).

    sudo /opt/builder/libexec/review-launch --repo <owner/name> --pr <N> \\
        --role {reviewer,security-reviewer} --run-id <id>

As root: mints a read-only installation token from the approver App's key
(`etc/approver-app.pem`, owned by root, mode 600 — never read by `agents` or
`reviewers`), fetches the PR and the diff pinned to its head, and writes
both to the run directory as files. Builds the session's persona and task
itself (`review_launch.build_prompt`) from what it just fetched — nothing
here comes from this process's own argv, environment or inherited stdin —
and runs it as the `reviewers` account (`ClaudeSession`, below) from a
fresh, per-run `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` with `--setting-sources user`, so a PR
that ships a `SessionStart` hook cannot run it. Reads the session's verdict
back from ITS OWN stdout (round 4 of #732, finding 2 — never a file under
the run directory; see `review_launch.extract_verdict`'s docstring), only if
that session itself exited 0, re-reads the PR's current base, mints a
write-scoped token, posts the PR comment and (only when the base was `main`
at both fetch and post) the `builder/review/<role>` check, and revokes both
tokens.

ALL ORCHESTRATION LOGIC IS IN `review_launch.py` (`lib/agent-platform/`).
This file supplies the real GitHub calls, the real key path and a real
`claude -p` subprocess; every test fakes all three, so no test here touches
the approver key, runs `sudo`, or makes a network call.

THE SHEBANG IS AN ABSOLUTE PATH, NOT `#!/usr/bin/env python3.11` (round 2 of
#732, finding 5): `env` resolves `python3.11` by searching `PATH`, and this
script is reached only through `sudo`, whose `env_reset` (kept — platform/
specs/builder-runs-as-two-accounts Handoff) sets `PATH` from sudoers'
`secure_path` if one is configured and otherwise falls back to a default
that this script must not depend on either way — the resolution should not
hinge on how that separate file ends up configured. `libexec/review-launch`
installs byte-for-byte from agent-platform's `main` with no per-platform
rewrite (contrast `libexec/update-privileged`, whose exec line IS rewritten
per platform at install time), so the path committed here is the one that
has to be correct on the install it actually runs on. `tests/
test_bin_shebangs.py`'s `ALLOWED` carries this exemption next to
`co-session-health`'s, for the same reason: neither file is ever symlinked
into `~/.local/bin` across arbitrary machines the way an ordinary `bin/`
command is.

THE `-I` IS ROUND 3 OF #732's ADDITION TO THAT SAME SHEBANG (finding 2): it
puts the interpreter in isolated mode — no `PYTHONPATH`, no user site
directory, `sys.path` built from the script's own location only — which
matters here specifically because this interpreter (`/opt/homebrew/bin/
python3`) is owned by the OPERATOR's account, not root's (platform/decisions/
0090's catch: "Root runs an interpreter the operator's account owns...the
`admin` group can write its `bin`"). `-I` does not change who owns the
binary itself, but it does stop a `PYTHONPATH` or a `~/.local/lib/python*/
site-packages` entry — either one plantable by anything in `admin`, which
`agents` is not, so this is belt-and-suspenders rather than this finding's
main fix — from being consulted by a process that runs as root.

THIS PROCESS'S ENTIRE ENVIRONMENT IS REPLACED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IMPORTS
(round 3 of #732, finding 2; moved earlier in round 4, finding 5): `sudo`'s
`env_reset` (kept) decides what survives from the caller only via
`/etc/sudoers`, which this script must not have to trust either way — see
the shebang note above for the same argument applied to `PATH`
specifically. At round 3's head this replacement was `main()`'s first act,
AFTER `import review_launch as RL` below — but that import's own chain
(`review_protocol` -> `telemetry` -> `builder_root.runs_root()`) reads
`BUILDER_ROOT` from `os.environ` at IMPORT time, before `main()` ever got a
chance to replace it. `sudo`'s `env_reset` already drops that variable
before this process even starts, so nothing was exploitable through it
either way (confirmed: round 4's security review read the import chain and
found no effect), but the read should not have to depend on that separate
file's configuration any more than the shebang's absolute path does. The
replacement now runs guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":`, ahead of the
`import review_launch` below, rather than unconditionally at module scope —
this file is also loaded as an ordinary module by the test suite (`tests/
conftest.py`'s `review_launch_bin` fixture), which must not have its OWN
process environment wiped out as a side effect of importing a module under
test; `__name__` there is the module's given name, never `"__main__"`.
Clearing `os.environ` and replacing it with a fixed, minimal,
root-owned-paths-only dict before anything else runs means every subprocess
this process (or a module it imports) spawns without its own explicit
`env=` — today, only `github_app.app_jwt`'s `openssl` call — inherits that
fixed environment regardless of what `/etc/sudoers` does or does not keep.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import os
import pwd
import select
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

# Root-owned directories only; `/opt/homebrew` (the OPERATOR-owned
# interpreter's own tree) is deliberately not on it. Defined ahead of the
# `import review_launch` below — see the module docstring's "THIS PROCESS'S
# ENTIRE ENVIRONMENT IS REPLACED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IMPORTS".
_ROOT_ENV = {"PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin", "HOME": "/var/root"}

if __name__ == "__main__":
    os.environ.clear()
    os.environ.update(_ROOT_ENV)

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))

import review_launch as RL  # noqa: E402

OWNER = "convivy"

# `/opt/builder` is root-owned and fixed (platform/specs/builder-runs-as-two-
# accounts Handoff: "Root-owned tree /opt/builder"), never home-relative: this
# script is reachable only through the one sudo rule
# (`agents ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/builder/libexec/review-launch`), so it
# always runs as root, and `builder_root.real_account_home()` on that path
# would answer root's own home (`/var/root` on macOS) — a different, wrong
# location every time, never the tree Shipwright's installer actually
# populates. Contrast `bin/pr-state-capture`'s KEY_PATH, which is genuinely
# home-relative because the `agents` account's own key lives under its home.
KEY_PATH = Path("/opt/builder/etc/approver-app.pem")

# The reviewers' own Claude token (platform/specs/builder-runs-as-two-accounts
# Handoff: "root-owned, exported only into the session").
CLAUDE_TOKEN_PATH = Path("/opt/builder/etc/reviewers-claude-token")

# The account the reviewer session runs as (platform/decisions/0090's second
# account). Round 2 of #732, finding 2: `ClaudeSession` below drops to this
# account before `claude -p` ever starts — this script itself runs as root
# (from `sudo`), but nothing a reviewer session does should.
REVIEWERS_ACCOUNT = "reviewers"

# How much of the session's stdout `ClaudeSession.run` asks the pipe for in
# one `read()` call. Deliberately smaller than `RL.MAX_SESSION_STDOUT_BYTES`
# so the bound below (`min(_READ_CHUNK, max_bytes + 1 - total)`) can shrink
# the final request to just enough to detect an overflow by one byte,
# rather than always asking for a full chunk past the cap.
_READ_CHUNK = 65536


def _readable(stream, timeout: float) -> bool:
    """True once `stream` has data (or EOF) ready, or `timeout` seconds have
    passed with nothing — the default `select_fn` `ClaudeSession.run` polls,
    so its read loop never blocks longer than the time still left before
    `RL.SESSION_TIMEOUT_S` (round 5 of #732) WAITING FOR READINESS. `select`
    itself was never the gap round 6 of #732 closes — see `ClaudeSession.run`'s
    docstring for the read call that followed it instead. Injected the same
    way `getpwnam`/`popen` already are, so a test can fake session output
    without a real pipe or process — see `tests/test_review_launch_bin.py`."""
    ready, _, _ = select.select([stream], [], [], timeout)
    return bool(ready)


def _kill_process_group(proc) -> None:
    """Kills `proc`'s entire process group, not just `proc`'s own pid (round
    6 of #732). `run` starts every session with `start_new_session=True`
    precisely so this is possible without also signalling THIS (root)
    process's own group: a `claude -p` session can fork a grandchild that
    inherits the stdout pipe and never leaves the leader's own process
    group (no `setsid` of its own), and killing only the named child would
    leave that grandchild running (and the pipe open) behind it.

    ROUND 8 OF #732, THE SECURITY REVIEWER'S ROUND-7 FINDING CORRECTS A
    FALSE CLAIM THIS DOCSTRING AND `ClaudeSession.run`'S BOTH USED TO MAKE:
    this kill does NOT reach "a tool-call subprocess the session spawned".
    On the reviewer's machine (Claude Code 2.1.287), the Bash tool starts
    each command it runs in ITS OWN process group, distinct from `claude`'s
    — `os.killpg(proc.pid, ...)` never reaches a Bash-tool command or
    anything that command backgrounds, on any exit path. What this kill
    actually reaches is the session leader and any descendant that stays in
    the LEADER's group, such as a plain `os.fork()` child that never starts
    a session of its own — the case the paragraph above describes.
    Containment that also covered a Bash-tool command would have to hold by
    uid (ending every process the `reviewers` account owns), which is a
    design question for `platform/specs/builder-runs-as-two-accounts`, not
    a round-8 code change — a uid-based kill would also end a concurrent
    review sharing that account, which the spec currently accepts.

    ROUND 7 OF #732, THE SECURITY REVIEWER'S ROUND-6 FINDING: at round 6's
    head (e41fcdca) this called `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGKILL)` —
    looking up `pid`'s OWN pgid before signalling it, rather than using the
    pgid this process already knows (`start_new_session=True` makes it
    equal `proc.pid`, for the life of the group). On macOS, `os.getpgid(pid)`
    raises `ProcessLookupError` once `pid` — the leader — has exited, EVEN
    WHILE another member of the same group is still alive: the group's
    existence does not depend on the one process whose pid happens to equal
    its pgid still being around to answer a lookup about itself. The
    reviewer reproduced this directly (`ps -g` showing the leader
    `<defunct>` with a `sleep` still alive in the same group) and confirmed
    `os.killpg(proc.pid, SIGKILL)` — skipping the lookup — killed it. This
    now calls `killpg` on `proc.pid` directly; `ProcessLookupError` from
    THAT call (never from a `getpgid` lookup, which no longer happens) means
    the whole group, leader included, is already gone, which is simply
    nothing left to signal.

    Tolerates a fake `proc` with no real `pid` (the test doubles in `tests/
    test_review_launch_bin.py`) via the `getattr` below. `proc.kill()`
    still runs afterward — every test double implements it, and for a real
    `proc` it is a harmless, redundant signal to a pid `killpg` above
    already covers (the leader is always a member of its own group)."""
    pid = getattr(proc, "pid", None)
    if pid is not None:
        try:
            os.killpg(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except (ProcessLookupError, OSError):
            pass
    proc.kill()


class _Terminated(Exception):
    """Raised by `_handle_terminate` so a `SIGTERM`, `SIGHUP` or `SIGINT`
    this (root) process receives unwinds through the SAME `finally` blocks
    an ordinary exception would, rather than ending the process immediately
    with none of them run (round 7 of #732, finding 3). `ClaudeSession.run`'s
    own `finally` already kills the session's process group and bounds the
    closing `wait()` on any exception raised while it is blocked in
    `self._select` or the read — this makes a signal one of those, instead
    of a case neither `run` nor `RL.main`'s own token-revoking `finally`
    blocks ever see. `agentctl kill` reaches this process by having `sudo`
    forward the SIGTERM a plain `kill <pid>` sends; SIGHUP is handled the
    same way since nothing here gives it a different disposition than the
    default (terminate, no cleanup) that is exactly the gap this closes.

    ROUND 8 OF #732, THE CODE REVIEWER'S ROUND-7 FINDING CORRECTS WHAT THIS
    CLOSES FOR SIGINT SPECIFICALLY: CPython installs its own default SIGINT
    handler at interpreter startup, which already raises `KeyboardInterrupt`
    — itself unwinding through the same `try`/`finally` chains this handler
    does. Installing `_handle_terminate` for SIGINT does not close a
    cleanup gap SIGINT already had; it normalises SIGINT's exit code and
    exception type to match SIGTERM and SIGHUP's, so all three leave this
    process the same way."""


def _handle_terminate(signum, frame):  # noqa: ARG001 — frame unused; signal.signal requires it
    # Round 8 of #732, the security reviewer's round-7 finding 1 (the second
    # window): set all three to their default ignored disposition BEFORE
    # raising, so a second SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT that lands while `run`'s
    # `finally` is still unwinding the first one — anywhere in it, `os.
    # killpg` included — cannot re-enter this handler and replace the
    # exception already propagating with a fresh one. Only the signal that
    # gets here first ever unwinds anything; every later one is silently
    # dropped, which is exactly what should happen to a session that is
    # already on its way out.
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT):
        signal.signal(sig, signal.SIG_IGN)
    raise _Terminated(signum)


class ClaudeSession:
    """The real reviewer session. Round 2 of #732, finding 2: constructed as
    root, but `run` drops to `REVIEWERS_ACCOUNT` before `claude -p` ever
    starts, and `home()` reads that account's OWN passwd entry rather than
    this (root) process's `$HOME` or `os.path.expanduser("~")`, either of
    which would answer root's home instead. `stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL`
    (finding 1) is what stops the session from inheriting whatever is
    sitting on the sudo caller's own stdin — `review_launch.build_prompt`
    already supplies the session's entire task as the command's final
    argument, so there is nothing for stdin to usefully carry anyway.

    ROUND 3 OF #732, FINDING 1 CORRECTS A FALSE CLAIM THIS DOCSTRING USED TO
    MAKE: `subprocess.run(user=..., group=...)` does NOT "perform its own
    setgroups" — CPython only calls `setgroups()` when `extra_groups` is
    ALSO given; `user=`/`group=` alone only call `setreuid`/`setregid`, and
    the child keeps every supplementary group THIS (root) process belongs to.
    Root is in `admin` (platform/decisions/0090's catch: "`admin` group can
    write its `bin`" — this machine's `/opt/homebrew/bin`, this very
    interpreter's own directory), so without `extra_groups` the dropped-
    privilege reviewer session would still carry `admin` membership — able to
    plant code a FUTURE root-run `review-launch` would execute, the same
    class of escalation finding 2's `-I`/environment-lockdown closes from a
    different angle. `run` below passes `extra_groups=` computed from the
    `reviewers` account's OWN group list (`os.getgrouplist`, injected the
    same way `getpwnam`/`popen` already are), which is what actually makes
    `setgroups()` run — and run with THAT account's groups, not root's.

    ROUND 4 OF #732, FINDING 1 REMOVES THE `chown` INJECTION ENTIRELY: at
    round 3's head (09642bed) `run` itself chowned the run directory and
    every pre-existing entry in it to `self._account` before starting the
    subprocess — making the run directory WRITABLE by `reviewers` is the one
    precondition every attack the round-4 security review found (hardlinking
    a root-owned file into it, or renaming the whole directory away and
    leaving a symlink at its old name) needed in the first place.
    `review_launch.prepare_run_dir` now creates AND owns every entry the
    session needs — `pr.json`, `diff.patch`, `worktree/`, `claude/` — itself,
    by file descriptor, before `RL.main` ever calls this method, and leaves
    the run directory itself root-owned and non-writable by anyone else (see
    `prepare_run_dir`'s own docstring). `run` below creates nothing and
    chowns nothing; it only drops privilege and starts the subprocess.

    ROUND 5 OF #732, FINDING 2 REPLACES `subprocess.run(stdout=PIPE,
    timeout=...)` WITH `subprocess.Popen` AND `run`'S OWN BOUNDED READ LOOP:
    at round 4's head (309d2e38) `subprocess.run`'s `stdout=subprocess.PIPE`
    captured the ENTIRE child stdout into memory before returning at all —
    so `RL.MAX_SESSION_STDOUT_BYTES` only ever bounded what `RL.extract_verdict`
    would later refuse to PARSE, after root had already read and held an
    unbounded amount of a steered or runaway session's own output, which is
    not what that constant's own docstring claims it bounds. `run` below
    instead reads the child's stdout itself, a chunk at a time, enforcing
    both `RL.MAX_SESSION_STDOUT_BYTES` and the `RL.SESSION_TIMEOUT_S`
    deadline WHILE reading — the moment either is crossed, the session is
    killed and nothing further is read. `popen`, `select_fn` and `monotonic`
    are injected (defaulting to the real `subprocess.Popen`, `_readable`,
    and `time.monotonic`) so a test can drive the read loop deterministically
    — real session output and a real clock, without an actual pipe or
    process, or a real multi-second wait for a timeout.

    ROUND 6 OF #732, BOTH REVIEWS, SAME FINDING: round 5's read loop called
    `proc.stdout.read(n)` after `self._select` reported the pipe ready, but
    `Popen`'s default `bufsize=-1` makes `proc.stdout` an `io.BufferedReader`,
    and `BufferedReader.read(n)` does NOT return once any data has arrived —
    it keeps issuing raw reads until it has `n` bytes or hits EOF. A session
    that writes a little output and then stalls (a hung API connection, a
    steered child) left that one `.read()` call blocked past `remaining`,
    with the deadline only re-checked after it returned — never, for a
    session that never returns. Both reviews reproduced this against a real
    pipe. `run` below now starts the child with `bufsize=0`, so `proc.stdout`
    is a raw, unbuffered stream whose `.read(n)` is one `read(2)` syscall
    that returns as soon as ANY data is available, exactly the semantics
    `self._select` already assumed. The `finally` block also now kills
    `proc`'s whole process group (`_kill_process_group`, via
    `start_new_session=True` below), including an exception raised by
    `self._select` or the read itself, and bounds the closing `proc.wait()`
    by whatever of the deadline is left rather than waiting on it
    unboundedly.

    ROUND 7 OF #732 FIXES TWO THINGS BOTH REVIEWS' ROUND-6 FOLLOW-UP FOUND.

    FIRST: at round 6's head (e41fcdca) the `finally` block above killed the
    group only `if not clean_eof` — a session whose leader exits cleanly
    (closing its own end of the pipe, so `run` sees ordinary EOF) while a
    grandchild it spawned is still alive, and does not itself hold the pipe
    open, got NO kill at all; the grandchild and anything it does next
    (write into a LATER review's own run directory, say) simply keeps
    running. The group is now killed on every exit from the loop, clean EOF
    included — see `_kill_process_group`'s own docstring for the matching
    fix to how it signals that group, which both reviews reproduced
    together as one finding.

    SECOND: the closing `proc.wait()`'s budget used to be `max(0.0,
    remaining)`, where `remaining` is whatever the loop last computed —
    at the TOP of whatever iteration last ran, before that iteration's own
    `self._select` call (which can itself block for up to that same
    `remaining` seconds). A session whose pipe closes late in that blocked
    call reaches the `finally` block with `remaining` still holding close to
    the FULL value it had before the block, even though almost none of it
    is actually left — so the wait below could run for nearly another whole
    `SESSION_TIMEOUT_S` past the real deadline. `wait_budget` is now
    `max(0.0, deadline - self._monotonic())`, a FRESH read of the clock at
    the point of waiting, never the loop's last snapshot of it — the
    `max(0.0, ...)` clamp is exactly what makes a deadline already passed by
    the time this runs wait for `0`, not "wait again" the way round 6's own
    docstring wrongly argued a fresh clock read would.

    ROUND 8 OF #732, THE SECURITY REVIEWER'S ROUND-7 FOLLOW-UP FINDING
    CLOSES TWO SIGNAL WINDOWS ROUND 7 LEFT OPEN, BOTH ON THE PATH TO
    `_kill_process_group` EVER RUNNING AT ALL.

    FIRST, THE STARTUP WINDOW: at round 7's head (b9828a78) `self._popen(...)`
    ran with SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT at their round-7 disposition (the
    handler installed in `main`), and `proc` was not under any `try`/
    `finally` of `run`'s own until several lines later. A signal landing
    inside `Popen` itself (the reviewer measured ~1.2ms there) or in the
    gap right after it returns raised `_Terminated` with no `finally` yet
    able to see `proc`, leaving the session's whole process group running
    with nothing left to end it. `run` below now blocks all three signals
    with `signal.pthread_sigmask(SIG_BLOCK, ...)` before calling
    `self._popen`, passes a `preexec_fn` that unblocks them again in the
    CHILD before it execs (so the reviewer session is not left with these
    three permanently blocked), and unblocks them again in THIS process as
    the very FIRST statement inside the `try` whose `finally` kills
    `proc`'s group — not right after `Popen` returns, which would just
    reopen the same gap a few lines later. A signal that arrived while
    blocked is delivered the moment it is unblocked, which is now always
    already inside that `try`.

    SECOND, A SIGNAL DURING CLEANUP ITSELF: the handler stayed installed
    for the whole of `finally`'s own execution, so a second signal landing
    after the first one had already started unwinding — before `os.killpg`
    in `_kill_process_group` got to run — raised AGAIN from inside the
    still-executing `finally`, replacing the exception already propagating
    and abandoning the group kill it was in the middle of. `_handle_
    terminate` now sets all three signals to `SIG_IGN` as its own first
    act, before it raises — so only the signal that gets there first ever
    unwinds anything, and nothing after it can interrupt the cleanup that
    first signal's `_Terminated` is already running.

    Both windows are exercised with real subprocesses and a deterministic
    hook rather than a race against a timer: `after_popen=` and
    `before_group_kill=` (both test-only, no-ops by default) let a test
    fire a signal from exactly the point each finding names — see `tests/
    test_review_launch_bin.py`.

    `getpwnam` and `popen` are injected (defaulting to the real `pwd`/
    `subprocess` calls) so a test can fake the account and the subprocess
    without being root or having a `reviewers` account to look up — see
    `tests/test_review_launch_bin.py`."""

    def __init__(self, *, oauth_token_path: Path, account: str = REVIEWERS_ACCOUNT,
                getpwnam=pwd.getpwnam, popen=subprocess.Popen, getgrouplist=os.getgrouplist,
                select_fn=_readable, monotonic=time.monotonic,
                after_popen=lambda proc: None, before_group_kill=lambda: None):
        self._oauth_token_path = oauth_token_path
        self._account = account
        self._pwent = getpwnam(account)
        self._popen = popen
        self._getgrouplist = getgrouplist
        self._select = select_fn
        self._monotonic = monotonic
        # Round 8 of #732: test-only, no-op-by-default hooks that let a
        # test fire a signal from exactly the startup window or exactly the
        # top of `finally`, rather than racing a timer against either one —
        # see `run`'s own docstring and `tests/test_review_launch_bin.py`.
        self._after_popen = after_popen
        self._before_group_kill = before_group_kill

    def home(self) -> Path:
        return Path(self._pwent.pw_dir)

    def owner(self) -> "tuple[int, int]":
        """`(uid, gid)` for `self._account` — what `review_launch.main`
        passes to `review_launch.prepare_run_dir` (round 4 of #732, finding
        1) so root chowns the run's fixed children to the RIGHT account
        without this module needing to look it up a second time."""
        return (self._pwent.pw_uid, self._pwent.pw_gid)

    def env(self, *, claude_config_dir: Path, home: Path) -> "dict[str, str]":
        try:
            token = self._oauth_token_path.read_text().strip()
        except OSError:
            token = ""
        return RL.session_env(claude_config_dir=claude_config_dir, oauth_token=token, home=home)

    def run(self, *, cmd: "list[str]", env: "dict[str, str]", cwd: Path) -> "tuple[int, str | None]":
        """Runs `cmd` as `self._account` and reads the session's verdict
        from its OWN stdout (round 4 of #732, finding 2) — never a file
        under `cwd`'s run directory; see `review_launch.extract_verdict`'s
        docstring for why. `review_launch.prepare_run_dir` has already
        created and owned `cwd` and its siblings by the time this runs; this
        method creates nothing and chowns nothing.

        `extra_groups=` is what makes `setgroups()` actually run in the
        child (see the class docstring's finding-1 correction) — without it
        the child keeps every supplementary group THIS (root) process
        belongs to, `admin` included.

        ROUND 5 OF #732, FINDING 2: this no longer hands the whole run to
        `subprocess.run(timeout=...)` and reads `.stdout` from the completed
        result — that buffers every byte the child ever writes before this
        method sees any of them. Instead, `self._popen` starts the child
        with `stdout=subprocess.PIPE`, and the loop below reads it a chunk
        (`_READ_CHUNK` bytes) at a time, tracking a running total and a
        deadline (`self._monotonic() + RL.SESSION_TIMEOUT_S`, read once, up
        front). Each read is capped at `max_bytes + 1 - total` — never more
        than one byte past `RL.MAX_SESSION_STDOUT_BYTES` — so an oversized or
        runaway session's output is detected, and the session killed, before
        this process ever buffers meaningfully more than the cap.

        ROUND 6 OF #732, BOTH REVIEWS: round 5's claim that "a session that
        has gone quiet is caught by `self._select` returning false for the
        time remaining" was false for a session that writes a LITTLE output
        and then goes quiet — `self._select` correctly reports the pipe
        ready, but the `proc.stdout.read(...)` call that followed it was a
        `BufferedReader.read(n)` (the `Popen` default, `bufsize=-1`), which
        keeps reading until it has `n` bytes or hits EOF, not until it has
        ANY bytes. That one call could then block past `remaining` with no
        way back to the top of the loop to notice. `self._popen` below is
        now given `bufsize=0`, so `proc.stdout` is a raw, unbuffered stream:
        once `self._select` reports it ready, `.read(n)` is one `read(2)`
        syscall that returns with whatever is available (1 byte or more),
        never waiting for a full chunk. `start_new_session=True` makes the
        child its own process group leader, so the `finally` block below can
        kill that whole group (`_kill_process_group`) — covering a forked
        grandchild that never leaves the leader's group, not just the
        session's own pid (see `_kill_process_group`'s own docstring, round
        8 of #732, for what this reaches and does not) — including an
        exception raised by `self._select` or the read itself
        (the exception is not swallowed: `finally` just makes sure cleanup
        happens before it keeps propagating). If the bounded wait below
        itself times out, the group is killed again and the final
        `proc.wait()` has no bound left to give, since the group is by then
        dead or dying. Either bound crossed discards whatever was read: a
        killed session reports exit code 124 (timeout) or 125 (output past
        the cap) with no verdict, which `RL.main` already treats as "no
        verdict trusted" the same way any other non-zero exit does.

        ROUND 7 OF #732 CLOSES TWO GAPS BOTH REVIEWS' ROUND-6 FOLLOW-UP FOUND
        — see `_kill_process_group`'s and this class's own docstring for the
        full account of each. FIRST: the group kill below no longer runs
        only `if not clean_eof` — a session whose own leader exits cleanly,
        leaving a grandchild it spawned alive in the group, used to get no
        kill at all; it now runs on every exit from the loop, clean EOF
        included. SECOND: `wait_budget` is now computed from a fresh
        `self._monotonic()` call at the point of waiting, `max(0.0, ...)`-
        clamped, rather than reused from `remaining`'s last value from the
        loop — which could be stale by nearly a full `self._select` call's
        worth of time if the pipe closed late inside that call, making the
        wait below run for close to another whole `RL.SESSION_TIMEOUT_S`
        past the real deadline. A SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGINT this process
        receives while blocked in `self._select` or the read raises
        `_Terminated` (module-level, installed in `main` below) through the
        exact same `finally` path — the signal is one more way an exception
        can interrupt this loop, not a separate mechanism.

        ROUND 8 OF #732 CLOSES TWO MORE SIGNAL WINDOWS — see `ClaudeSession`'s
        own docstring for the full account of both. FIRST: all three signals
        are now blocked (`signal.pthread_sigmask(SIG_BLOCK, ...)`) before
        `self._popen` is even called, and unblocked again only as the first
        statement inside the `try` below — never right after `self._popen`
        returns, which is exactly the gap that used to leave `proc` started
        but unprotected. The `preexec_fn` passed to `self._popen` unblocks
        them again in the CHILD before it execs, so the reviewer session
        itself does not inherit them blocked. SECOND: `_handle_terminate`
        now disables all three signals (`SIG_IGN`) before it ever raises,
        so a second signal arriving anywhere in the `finally` below —
        including inside `_kill_process_group` or either `proc.wait()` —
        cannot re-enter the handler and abandon the group kill partway
        through."""
        gid = self._pwent.pw_gid
        extra_groups = self._getgrouplist(self._account, gid)
        block_signals = (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT)
        # Round 8 of #732, the security reviewer's round-7 finding 1 (the
        # startup window): block these three around `Popen` itself, so a
        # signal that lands during it, or between it returning and the
        # `try:` below taking ownership of `proc`, is deferred rather than
        # raised with no `finally` yet able to see `proc` at all.
        signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, block_signals)
        try:
            proc = self._popen(cmd, env=env, cwd=str(cwd), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               user=self._account, group=self._account,
                               extra_groups=extra_groups, bufsize=0,
                               start_new_session=True,
                               preexec_fn=lambda: signal.pthread_sigmask(
                                   signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, block_signals))
        except BaseException:
            # `self._popen` itself failed (never a signal — those are still
            # blocked) — nothing was started, but THIS process must not be
            # left with these three permanently blocked for the rest of its
            # life, so restore them before the failure propagates.
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, block_signals)
            raise
        # Round 8 of #732: a test-only, no-op-by-default hook fired exactly
        # here — `proc` exists, signals are still blocked, nothing protects
        # it yet — so a test can demonstrate the startup window without
        # racing a timer. See `ClaudeSession`'s own docstring.
        self._after_popen(proc)
        max_bytes = RL.MAX_SESSION_STDOUT_BYTES
        deadline = self._monotonic() + RL.SESSION_TIMEOUT_S
        chunks: "list[bytes]" = []
        total = 0
        timed_out = False
        overflowed = False
        try:
            # Round 8 of #732: unblocked here, as the FIRST statement inside
            # the try/finally that now owns `proc` — a signal still pending
            # from the blocked window above, or one that lands the instant
            # this call returns, is delivered at or after this point, which
            # is to say already inside this `try`, where the `finally`
            # below is already in effect.
            signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, block_signals)
            while True:
                remaining = deadline - self._monotonic()
                if remaining <= 0:
                    timed_out = True
                    break
                if not self._select(proc.stdout, remaining):
                    continue
                data = proc.stdout.read(min(_READ_CHUNK, max_bytes + 1 - total))
                if not data:
                    break  # EOF: the session's own process closed stdout
                total += len(data)
                chunks.append(data)
                if total > max_bytes:
                    overflowed = True
                    break
        finally:
            # Round 8 of #732: a test-only, no-op-by-default hook fired as
            # the first statement of this `finally`, before the group kill
            # — lets a test demonstrate a second signal landing during
            # cleanup without racing a timer. See `ClaudeSession`'s own
            # docstring.
            self._before_group_kill()
            # Round 7 of #732: killed on EVERY exit, clean EOF included —
            # the leader closing its own stdout cleanly says nothing about
            # whether a grandchild it spawned is still alive in the group.
            _kill_process_group(proc)
            # Round 7 of #732: a FRESH clock read, not the loop's last
            # `remaining` snapshot — see this method's own docstring.
            wait_budget = max(0.0, deadline - self._monotonic())
            try:
                proc.wait(timeout=wait_budget)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                _kill_process_group(proc)
                proc.wait()
            proc.stdout.close()

        if timed_out:
            return 124, None
        if overflowed:
            return 125, None
        return proc.returncode, RL.extract_verdict(b"".join(chunks), max_bytes=max_bytes)


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    argv = sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv
    # Round 7 of #732, finding 3: `agentctl kill` reaches this process as a
    # plain SIGTERM, which `sudo` forwards unchanged — with no handler
    # installed, the default disposition ends this (root) process at once,
    # running neither `ClaudeSession.run`'s process-group kill nor `RL.main`'s
    # own token-revoking `finally` blocks, so the session it started keeps
    # running, orphaned, with no deadline left to end it. Installing a
    # handler that raises `_Terminated` turns the signal into an ordinary
    # exception that unwinds through those SAME `finally` blocks instead.
    # SIGHUP gets the same handler for the same reason: nothing here gives
    # it a different, safer default than SIGTERM's. SIGINT also gets this
    # handler, but round 8 of #732 (the code reviewer's round-7 finding)
    # corrects what that closes: CPython's own default SIGINT handler
    # already raises KeyboardInterrupt, which already unwinds through the
    # same finally blocks — installing this handler for SIGINT normalises
    # its exit code and exception type to match the other two, it does not
    # close a cleanup gap SIGINT already had.
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT):
        signal.signal(sig, _handle_terminate)
    gate = RL.ProductionGate(key_path=KEY_PATH, app_id=RL.APPROVER_APP_ID, owner=OWNER,
                             opener=urllib.request.urlopen)
    session = ClaudeSession(oauth_token_path=CLAUDE_TOKEN_PATH)
    # Run directories live under the `reviewers` account's OWN home
    # (platform/specs/builder-runs-as-two-accounts Handoff: "Session start:
    # CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<REVIEWERS_ACCOUNT's home>/runs/<run-id>/claude"), never under
    # the root-owned `/opt/builder` tree — `review_launch.prepare_run_dir`
    # chowns `pr.json`/`diff.patch`/`worktree/`/`claude/` to that account
    # (never the run directory itself — round 4 of #732, finding 1) once
    # `ClaudeSession.owner()` tells it who to chown them to.
    try:
        return RL.main(argv, gate=gate, session=session,
                       run_dir_for=lambda run_id: session.home() / "runs" / run_id)
    except _Terminated as e:
        # The process-group kill and any token revocation already ran, via
        # the `finally` blocks the exception unwound through on its way
        # here. The conventional 128+signum exit status is all that is
        # left to report.
        return 128 + int(e.args[0])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
